<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaac Watts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1674">1674</a>–<a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaac Watts was an English Congregational minister, hymn writer, theologian and logician, credited with some 750 hymns, among them When I Survey the Wondrous Cross, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joy-to-the-world">Joy to the World</a> and Our God, Our Help in Ages Past. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> to a nonconformist family, he was barred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, then restricted to Anglicans, studied at the Dissenting Academy at Stoke Newington and became pastor of the Mark Lane Congregational Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His Hymns and Spiritual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> (1707) and his metrical psalter of 1719, which rendered the Psalms in the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, helped bring new hymns into English worship. His Logick (1724) was printed in twenty editions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-watts.jpg" alt="Isaac Watts" width="330" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isaac_Watts_from_NPG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Joy to the World&quot; was written by the English minister and hymn writer <b>Isaac Watts</b> as a Christian reading of Psalm 98, with an allusion to Genesis 3.</blockquote><a href="/songs/joy-to-the-world">Joy to the World</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Isaac Watts</b>'s paraphrase of Psalm 117 is sung to it, shape-note singers of the Sacred Harp use it for Tate and Brady's Psalm 95, and Hiram Bingham I translated a Hawaiian version, Hoʻonani i ka Makua mau.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Reformation the psalm was recast as metrical verse to be sung to hymn tunes: The Bay Psalm Book (1640), the Sidney Psalms, and settings by George Herbert and <b>Isaac Watts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li><li><blockquote>Its music carries an adaptation of <b>Isaac Watts</b>'s hymn &quot;When I Survey the Wondrous Cross&quot; in the Mennonite Hymnal: A Worship Book.</blockquote><a href="/songs/senzeni-na">Senzeni Na?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.91,-1.4&amp;year=1722" aria-label="Southampton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4518 398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isaac Watts"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4968 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4968 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.91,-1.4&amp;year=1722">What was here in 1722? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Our God, our help in ages past, Our hope for years to come, Our shelter from the stormy blast, And our eternal home.&#8221;</p><footer>Psalm 90, The Psalms of David Imitated (1719) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13166/pg13166-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When I survey the wondrous cross On which the Prince of Glory dy'd, My richest gain I count but loss, And pour contempt on all my pride.&#8221;</p><footer>When I Survey the Wondrous Cross, Hymns and Spiritual Songs (1707) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13341/pg13341-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;How doth the little busy bee Improve each shining hour, And gather honey all the day From every opening flower!&#8221;</p><footer>Against Idleness and Mischief, Divine Songs for Children (1715) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13439/pg13439-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Joy to the world; the Lord is come; Let earth receive her King; Let every heart prepare him room, And heaven and nature sing.&#8221;</p><footer>Psalm 98, The Psalms of David Imitated (1719) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13166/pg13166-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is a land of pure delight Where saints immortal reign, Infinite day excludes the night, And pleasures banish pain.&#8221;</p><footer>There Is a Land of Pure Delight, Hymns and Spiritual Songs (1707) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13341/pg13341-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
